Robotics needs more than better hardware. It needs better data.

@axisrobotics is crowdsourcing human trajectory data through browser-based simulations, creating a scalable data engine for next-gen physical AI.

The goal: turn human experience into training data robots can actually learn from.

There exists a striking similarity between the process of discovery and statistical mechanics, which has helped us build AI that explore and discover beyond their training data. It offers a first-principles view of adaptive AI, using powerful concepts from physics to unify seemingly disparate fields. I presented this and many other results at the Computational Materials Science and Engineering @GordonConf yesterday. Thank you Yaroslava Yingling and André Schleife for chairing this inspiring meeting  at @sundayriver. Incredible energy throughout the week and wonderful discussions with everyone including many junior researchers and rising stars. 

I'll be expanding on these ideas in a separate post, but here is a brief summary of the core arguments: Looking at total description length (model complexity plus unexplained evidence) as an effective potential over model space we can see how system can become trapped in a locally optimal representation space. Reaching a better one requires adding a new variable, mechanism, symmetry, PDE, or operator - temporarily increasing complexity before the new representation compresses the evidence more effectively. Then new evidence changes the relative stability of these models. We have shown how agents actively seek data that stress the current explanation and use the failures to construct a better world model. In protein mechanics, this led to a quantitative law coupling local elastic compliance with collective-mode participation. 

